'''Almuria''', or '''Al-Muriyah''' ([[wikipedia:Arabic language|Bayani]]: (بلاد المُرِّية); ''bilād al-murriyya'', lands of the [[Muri]]) is a country comprising of the continent of <>. At <> square kilometers, it is the <> largest country on [[Adonia]] and, with a population of over 53 million, Adonia's <> most populous. As an island nation, Almuria does not share any land or maritime borders with any other state.

Human populations settled in Almuria over 50,000 years ago. Mostly isolated from the rest of the globe, the indigenous peoples of Almuria mostly dwelled in [[wikipedia:nomad|semi-nomadic]] groups depending on the migration patterns of local fauna or seasonal food availability. Indigenous Almurians developed one of the oldest continuous cultures in Adonia history, developing uninterrupted since first being settled. With the spread of maritime trade routes, contact between [[Bayani]] and the indigenous was established in the [[The Great Contact|13th century]]. Indigenous Almurians traded with the Bayani extensively, exchanging agricultural goods grown in the fertile south with Bayani luxury goods. 

The Bayani-Almurian relationship changed in the 18th century with the rise of the [[Guild of Almuria]] that sought to reimpose trade with the indigenous after a [[Great Almurian Drought|half-decade long drought]] significantly lowered agricultural yields on the island. Bayani merchants whose [[The Five Families|families]] benefited greatly from trade with the indigenous, collaborated in the forced appropriation of indigenous lands, [[Slavery in Almuria|forcing them to work on their own land as slaves]]. The families invited Bayani people to settle in Almuria to strengthen their control. This prompted a century-long struggle between the Bayani and indigenous Almurians, though ultimately the Bayani were able to solidify their control in the 1784 [[Treaty of Dharuk]]. As a result of the struggle, the Bayani merchants began bringing in purchased slaves from [[Kaftia]]. 

The fall of the Bayani Empire in 1821 largely left Almuria isolated from its Bayani economic and political contacts that empowered the Almurian ruling class, effectively making Almuria independent. A period of economic and political instability devastated Almuria and the power of the Five Families declined dramatically. The power vacuum was quickly filled by [[Malik Al-Mazra'a| Bayani land and plantation owners]] who established a [[Almurian Directorate|constitutional directorate]] in 1827. The directorate saw minimal popular support and the directorate resorted to violence and draconian measures to subdue dissent, but was able to integrate Almuria into the [[Fosia|Fosian]] and Kaftian trade networks and economic stability came soon after. Despite its authoritarian nature, the directorate issued the [[Fatwa on Almurian Enslavement]] and abolished slavery in Almuria and the directorate sought to integrate the indigenous Almurians into state society to limited success, but was nevertheless successful in gaining some trust in the new ruling class from the population.

The discovery of coal deposits in Almuria marked the beginning of dramatic economic shifts in Almuria, which had been a predominantly agrarian society. Coal exploitation came shortly after the introduction of the [[wikipedia:steam engine|steam engine]] to Almuria which brought with it improvements in agricultural and production efficiency. Almuria became one of the most quickly industrializing nations in the early 20th century and, with it, further exacerbation of already abnormally high wealth inequality. Almuria became known as one of, if not the, most unequal nations on Adonia.

