==Etymology==

Named after the Muri people.

==History==
===Pre-history===

The first human inhabitants of modern-day Almuria are estimated to have arrived to the island around 50,000 years ago. The original human group that traveled to Almuria likely originated in modern-day [[Pasundan]] and island hopped across the <> Islands. The distance between the <> and <> archipelagos delayed the arrival of humans to Almuria. Studies of the genetics of [[Indigenous Almurians|indigenous Almurians]] indicate that the size of the initial group amounted to 50-100 individuals, though some small groups arrived in Almuria through the same route, though these group largely remained in the southern coast. 

In the initial centuries after the arrival of humans, groups spread across the island and largely remained within 50-100 kilometers from their birth places. Nevertheless, the first humans lived as [[wikipedia:nomad|semi-nomadic]] societies, traveling to different environments depending on the season and food availability. These groups largely relied on hunting [[Fauna of Almuria|large game]] and native [[Flora of Almuria|plants]]. Some larger fauna, such as the [[Almurian elephant]], were hunted to extinction by ten to twenty thousand years ago. The loss of the Almurian elephant seems to have resulted in a cultural shift in indigenous society towards a more responsible model of [[wikipedia:wildlife management|wildlife management]] that sought to maintain the populations of hunted game to ensure a more stable food supply. Similar shifts in the management of plant life occurred earlier with the spread of the practice of [[wikipedia:Controlled burn|controlled burns]] and seeding of new forests. 

Due to the isolated nature of tribes, Almuria became host to a [[Languages of Almuria|wide variety of languages]] and cultures, but they all shared similar features, especially with regards to religion, which developed in reaction to the historical developments of the island, particularly the aforementioned loss of certain species of [[wikipedia:megafauna|megafauna]], which studies suggest resulted in food insecurity and significant population decline, indicating a tumultuous period of time for the indigenous. It's notable that many indigenous tribes have stories that describe a time of great insecurity, seen as punishment for the loss of a large animal that humanity had betrayed and destroyed that matches the description of the Almurian elephant based on archeological findings.

Virtually all Almurian populations believe in [[Alcherinqa (Religion)|Alcherinqa]], or "the Dreaming", "Dreamtime", a term which the Bayani later used to name the island continent. The concept of Alcherinqa placed tremendous emphasis on [[wikipedia:dream|dreams]], which was considered to be the primary connection between humankind and the supernatural. Alcherinqa is infamous due to its difficulty to explain in non-indigenous languages. Nevertheless, it's often explained as a perception of reality that transcends time and the physical, viewing human life experiences as a temporary state of being in which humans are tasked to navigate a world hostile to the prolonged continuation of a single life. The physical world is viewed as one side of a dichotomy, the other being the dream state, which is either viewed as the primary experience or a second but equal state of being, one which continues after the death of a human. However, the nature of an individual's dream-state is determined by their actions in the physical world, especially pertaining to their relationship to other forms of life, especially animals. The hunting of animals is seen as an unfortunate necessity, and many local rituals developed that sought to respect and recognize the value of the life of the hunted animal. Great emphasis was thus placed on living in harmony with nature. 

Due to the relatively late adoption of writing by the indigenous population, much of Almuria's history before the arrival of Bayani merchants is unknown. However, archeological evidence suggests constant evolution and propagation of ideas between tribes. The indigenous Almurians used [[wikipedia:story-telling|stories]] and [[wikipedia:singing|song]] to tell history. Many stories and songs have survived into the present day and even distant tribes may share similar songs and stories, confirming that despite the relative isolation of groups, contact with other tribes occurred on a regular basis.

Technology in Almuria did not change significantly before the arrival of Bayani merchants. [[wikipedia:metalworking|Manipulation of metals]] did not develop here, nor did [[wikipedia:writing|writing]] or [[wikipedia:sailing|sailing]]. Pressures that encouraged the development of these technologies elsewhere were not present in Almuria. Nevertheless, [[wikipedia:woodworking|woodworking]] and [[wikipedia:weaving|weaving]] in Almuria have rich histories and techniques developed by the indigenous were not developed elsewhere. Thus, it would not be reasonable to conclude that indigenous societies were stagnant or static as was once believed.

